### 🟠 BUG-06: [QuotesProvider](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/live/QuotesProvider.tsx#97-375) — `scheduleFlush` Captures Stale `quoteFlushMs`

**File:** [QuotesProvider.tsx L171-175](file:///wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/live/QuotesProvider.tsx#L171-L175)

```typescript
const scheduleFlush = useCallback(() => {
  pendingFlushRef.current = true;
  if (flushTimerRef.current !== null) return;
  flushTimerRef.current = window.setTimeout(flushNow, quoteFlushMs);  // ← captures quoteFlushMs
}, [flushNow, quoteFlushMs]);  // ← recalculated when quoteFlushMs changes
```

**Root cause:** When `quoteFlushMs` changes (e.g., tier changes → new flush interval), the `scheduleFlush` callback is recreated. But if a flush timer is **already scheduled** from the old closure, it fires with the **old** delay. The guard `if (flushTimerRef.current !== null) return` prevents a new timer from being set with the new interval.

**Scenario:** User on WiFi (INSTANT → 50ms flush). Network degrades to 3G. New `quoteFlushMs` = 1000ms. But the already-scheduled 50ms timer fires first. After it fires, the next `scheduleFlush` call uses the correct 1000ms. The opposite case (slow → fast) is worse: the user waits up to 1000ms for a flush that should now complete in 50ms.

**Impact:** One stale flush interval per tier transition. Low practical impact but correctness issue.

**Fix:** When `quoteFlushMs` changes, cancel the existing timer and reschedule:
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  if (flushTimerRef.current !== null && pendingFlushRef.current) {
    window.clearTimeout(flushTimerRef.current);
    flushTimerRef.current = window.setTimeout(flushNow, quoteFlushMs);
  }
}, [quoteFlushMs, flushNow]);
```

---

### 🟠 BUG-07: [prefetchAllRoutes](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/routePrefetch.ts#111-140) — `scheduled` Flag Never Resets, Blocks Re-Prefetch

**File:** [routePrefetch.ts L113-123](file:///wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/routePrefetch.ts#L111-L123)

```typescript
export function prefetchAllRoutes(options) {
  if (!prefetchEnabled() || typeof window === "undefined") return;
  if (scheduled) return;  // ← once true, never false again
  // ...
  scheduled = true;
```

But in [AuthenticatedShell.tsx](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/AuthenticatedShell.tsx):
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  prefetchAllRoutes({ hints: perfHints, settings: perfSettings, startDelayMs });
}, [perfHints, perfSettings]);  // ← re-runs every time hints/settings change
```

**Root cause:** `scheduled = true` is set on first call and never reset. When `perfHints` or `perfSettings` change (e.g., user comes online after being offline, or admin changes settings), the `useEffect` re-runs, calls [prefetchAllRoutes](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/routePrefetch.ts#111-140) again, but the `scheduled` guard blocks it.

**Impact:** If a user starts on a slow network (CONSTRAINED → 3 chunks), then upgrades to fast WiFi (INSTANT → 9 parallel), the remaining 6 chunks are never prefetched. The tier-aware prefetching only applies to the **first** call.

**Fix:** Either (a) track which chunks were already prefetched and only prefetch new ones, or (b) reset `scheduled` when the plan changes materially.

---

### 🟠 BUG-08: [pollIntervalForTier](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#378-409) + [tierPollIntervalMs](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#442-452) — Double [resolvePerformanceSettings](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#295-377) Call

**File:** [perfHints.ts L442-451](file:///wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#L442-L451)

```typescript
export function tierPollIntervalMs(baseMs, hints, settingsInput) {
  const settings = resolvePerformanceSettings(settingsInput);  // 1st resolve
  const configuredBase = clamp(Math.round(baseMs || settings.restFallbackPollMs), 100, 60_000);
  const hasSettingsInput = Boolean(settingsInput && typeof settingsInput === "object");
  return pollIntervalForTier(hints.networkTier, configuredBase, hasSettingsInput ? settingsInput : undefined);
  //                                                            ↑ passes raw settingsInput
}

export function pollIntervalForTier(tier, baseMs, settingsInput) {
  const normalizedTier = normalizeTier(tier);
  const base = clamp(Math.round(baseMs), 100, 60_000);
  const settings = resolvePerformanceSettings(settingsInput);  // 2nd resolve ⚠️
```

**Root cause:** [resolvePerformanceSettings(settingsInput)](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#295-377) is called twice — once in [tierPollIntervalMs](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#442-452) and once in [pollIntervalForTier](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#378-409). Same for [tierFlushIntervalMs](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#453-465) → [flushIntervalForTier](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#410-441). This is ~50 property accesses + clamps × 2 per call.

**Impact:** Performance waste. Not a correctness bug, but every poll interval computation does double the work. With 4 polling hooks, each calling this on every render, that's 8 unnecessary [resolvePerformanceSettings](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#295-377) calls per render cycle.

