### 🟡 BUG-10: `use-performance-settings` — Reads from `/api/global-settings` but PerformanceSettings Fields Don't Exist

**File:** [use-performance-settings.ts L10-19](file:///wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/hooks/use-performance-settings.ts#L10-L19)

```typescript
const query = useQuery({
  queryKey: ["/api/global-settings"],
  staleTime: 30_000,
  refetchOnWindowFocus: false,
});

return useMemo(() => {
  if (!query.data) return DEFAULT_PERFORMANCE_SETTINGS;
  return resolvePerformanceSettings(query.data);  // ← query.data has no perf fields
}, [query.data]);
```

**Root cause:** The `/api/global-settings` endpoint currently returns settings like `localeSuffix`, `supportedLanguages`, etc. — none of the [PerformanceSettings](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#7-25) fields (`restFallbackPollMs`, `pollInstantMs`, etc.) exist in the response. [resolvePerformanceSettings()](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#295-377) tolerates missing fields via [numOrNull(candidate.xxx) ?? default](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/bcodex/TD.2.ANTIGRAVITY/client/src/lib/perfHints.ts#106-110), so it always returns defaults.

**Impact:** Currently zero — defaults are correct. But it's misleading because:
1. Every consumer file imports and passes `performanceSettings` as if it's dynamic
2. It creates the false impression that admin-configured values are being used
3. There's a 30s `staleTime` refetch cycle for data that's never read

